<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Thamud</h1><p class="st-meta">715 BCE – 450 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An ancient Arab people of the Hejaz, known from Assyrian annals to the Quran, whose name lives on in thousands of rock inscriptions.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Thamud are first named among Arab groups defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> in 715 BCE. They lived in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hejaz">Hejaz</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafud">Nafud</a>, and later Greek and Roman writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny</a> and Ptolemy also place them there.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They seem to have been a confederation of herding and oasis tribes rather than a centralised kingdom. Their name was given by modern scholars to a large body of Ancient North Arabian graffiti, though not all were written by Thamud. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruwafa-inscriptions">Ruwafa</a>, Thamudic tribesmen built a temple in the 160s CE honouring the Roman emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-verus">Lucius Verus</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 400 CE Roman lists record a cavalry unit of Thamudeni in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, after which they disappear. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> tells of the Thamud as a people destroyed for rejecting the prophet <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/salihids">Salih</a>, and links them to the rock tombs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hegra">Hegra</a>, which were in fact Nabataean.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 715 BCE – 450 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Arabia and the Gulf</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:45.47%;width:23.18%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>715 BCE – 450 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Arabian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient North Arabian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Thamudeni</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-132&amp;focus=thamud" aria-label="Thamud on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5860 1298 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Thamud lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0M5919 1327h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6180 1554h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6180 1554h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Saudi Arabia. <a href="/atlas?year=-132&amp;focus=thamud">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

